@Power-up mode lookup tables: relocate three 10-entry tables to 11-entry copies so
@ power-up mode 10 has a real slot, and repoint the readers at the copies.
@
@ ===========================================================================
@ RESTORED 2026-10-22 after a corrected analysis. Read this before editing.
@ ===========================================================================
@
@ These hooks were briefly removed because I misread them. I recorded the six reader
@ words as "pointing live lookups at executable code", treating their stored values as
@ absolute addresses. They are not addresses. They are PC-relative offsets, used by the
@ standard ARM literal-pool idiom:
@
@     LDR Rn, =(table - pc)
@     LDR Rn, [PC, Rn]              ; Rn = table address
@
@ so `stored + pc = table`. Applying that relation:
@
@     site 0x78C58C  retail 0x0037FCF0 + pc 0x0078C540 = 0x00B0C230  real body table
@                    ours   0x003BCE98 + pc 0x0078C540 = 0x00B493D8  relocated body table
@
@ All six resolve exactly, and the relocated tables below match the reference build
@ byte-for-byte (verified for height, body, balloon and tall-actor). The original hooks
@ were correct in both bytes and meaning. The error was mine, in the analysis.
@
@ WHY THE RELOCATION IS NEEDED AT ALL
@ -----------------------------------
@ Three tables are indexed by the power-up mode and have exactly 10 entries, modes 0..9:
@
@     body     0xB0C230  [1,0,0,1,2,3,0,4,4,5]
@     height   0xB0C2BC  [16,31,31,6,31,31,31,31,31,31]
@     balloon  0xB0C34C  [0,-4,-4,4,-5,-2,-4,-4,-4,0]
@
@ A fourth, reached through a variable-index getter at 0x60796A60, starts at 0xB0C300:
@
@     tall     0xB0C300  [1,2,2,0,2,2,2,2,2,2]
@
@ Mode 10 reads index 10 of each. In retail that lands on whatever follows the table:
@ body[10] = 165 (not a valid body type), height[10] = 0.0. Extending in place is
@ impossible -- the words after each table belong to something else -- so the reference
@ build relocated copies and repointed the readers. We do the same, at the same RVAs.
@
@ ELEVEN ENTRIES EACH, mode 10 last:
@
@     height  0xB493A8 ... mode 10 = 31.0
@     body    0xB493D8 ... mode 10 = 0    (body type 0 = Normal)
@     balloon 0xB49404 ... mode 10 = -4.0
@     tall    0xB0C328     mode 10 = 2    (this one IS extended in place: it is the
@                                          spare word right after the 10 entries)
@
@ TYPE: everything below is `data`. The six reader words are DCD literals, not
@ instructions; tagging them `instr` worked only because the assembler happened to
@ reproduce the same bytes. `data` removes that round-trip risk entirely.

@ --- reader repoints: body table (three readers) ---------------------------
body_reader_a:
    @ 0x78C58C: DCD 0x00B493D8 - 0x0078C540
    type:    data
    data:    98 CE 3B 00
    address: 0x0078C58C

body_reader_b:
    @ 0x78E604: DCD 0x00B493D8 - 0x0078E5C0
    type:    data
    data:    18 AE 3B 00
    address: 0x0078E604

body_reader_c:
    @ 0x78E7E8: DCD 0x00B493D8 - 0x0078E630
    type:    data
    data:    A8 AD 3B 00
    address: 0x0078E7E8

@ --- reader repoints: height table (two readers) --------------------------
height_reader_a:
    @ 0x795FF0: DCD 0x00B493A8 - 0x00795D44
    type:    data
    data:    64 36 3B 00
    address: 0x00795FF0

height_reader_b:
    @ 0x7960F8: DCD 0x00B493A8 - 0x00796018
    type:    data
    data:    90 33 3B 00
    address: 0x007960F8

@ --- reader repoint: balloon offset table ---------------------------------
balloon_reader:
    @ 0x7982D8: DCD 0x00B49404 - 0x00798224
    type:    data
    data:    E0 11 3B 00
    address: 0x007982D8

@ --- relocated tables: 11 entries each, mode 10 last --------------
@ Bytes below were read directly out of convertedmain.hammer_b.elf at these
@ RVAs, not transcribed by hand. That matters: an earlier hand-written version
@ had four wrong bytes in the balloon table and over-ran its span.

@ height table @ 0x00b493a8  (modes 0..9 identical to retail; mode 10 = 31.0)
@   values: [16.0, 31.0, 31.0, 6.0, 31.0, 31.0, 31.0, 31.0, 31.0, 31.0, 31.0]
height_table_0:
    type:    data
    data:    00 00 80 41 00 00 F8 41 00 00 F8 41 00 00 C0 40
    address: 0x00b493a8

height_table_1:
    type:    data
    data:    00 00 F8 41 00 00 F8 41 00 00 F8 41 00 00 F8 41
    address: 0x00b493b8

height_table_2:
    type:    data
    data:    00 00 F8 41 00 00 F8 41 00 00 F8 41
    address: 0x00b493c8

@ body table @ 0x00b493d8  (modes 0..9 identical to retail; mode 10 = 0 (body type 0 = Normal))
@   values: [1, 0, 0, 1, 2, 3, 0, 4, 4, 5, 0]
body_table_0:
    type:    data
    data:    01 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 01 00 00 00
    address: 0x00b493d8

body_table_1:
    type:    data
    data:    02 00 00 00 03 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 04 00 00 00
    address: 0x00b493e8

body_table_2:
    type:    data
    data:    04 00 00 00 05 00 00 00 00 00 00 00
    address: 0x00b493f8

@ balloon table @ 0x00b49404  (modes 0..9 identical to retail; mode 10 = -4.0)
@   values: [0.0, -4.0, -4.0, 4.0, -5.0, -2.0, -4.0, -4.0, -4.0, 0.0, -4.0]
balloon_table_0:
    type:    data
    data:    00 00 00 00 00 00 80 C0 00 00 80 C0 00 00 80 40
    address: 0x00b49404

balloon_table_1:
    type:    data
    data:    00 00 A0 C0 00 00 00 C0 00 00 80 C0 00 00 80 C0
    address: 0x00b49414

balloon_table_2:
    type:    data
    data:    00 00 80 C0 00 00 00 00 00 00 80 C0
    address: 0x00b49424

@ --- tall-actor type: mode 10 extended IN PLACE --------------------------
tall_actor_type_hammer:
    @ 0xB0C328 is element [10] of the table at 0xB0C300 (base + 10*4), read via a
    @ variable-index getter at 0x60796A60 with no bounds check. Retail has 0 here;
    @ the Hammer build uses 2. It is the spare word after the ten real entries, so no
    @ relocation is needed for this one.
    type:    data
    data:    02 00 00 00
    address: 0x00B0C328

@ --- a gate that must also accept mode 10 --------------------------------
mode_six_or_ten:
    @ Retail e3500002 (cmp r0, #2) -> e350000a (cmp r0, #0xa).
    type:    instr
    instruction: cmp r0, #0xa
    address: 0x0078E6A8
